WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:07.020
Sol Ring is a Magic: the Gathering card. It was&nbsp;
first printed in 1993 in Alpha, the first print&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:11.580
run of the first Magic: the Gathering set, and&nbsp;
went on to be printed in the other three print&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:19.320
runs, ending in Spring of 1994. It is one of the&nbsp;
most powerful mana artifacts in the game, and has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.420
been banned or restricted in most constructed&nbsp;
formats for basically its entire existence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:30.960
It sat on the Reserved list, a list of cards that&nbsp;
Wizards of the Coast has promised not to reprint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:38.160
from 1996 until 2002 at which point Wizards&nbsp;
updated the reserved list to allow reprints&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.680
of the first set’s commons and uncommons.&nbsp;
The card commanded fairly high prices all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.540
through the 2000s, with it being reprinted&nbsp;
in the limited run From the Vault: Relics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:55.620
but still keeping most of its value. Then, it&nbsp;
was printed in the first commander set in 2011,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:01.260
as one of two cards included in all&nbsp;
five of the set’s preconstructed decks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:07.140
The other card was Command Tower,&nbsp;
effectively a downside-less Mana Confluence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.880
The reasoning for this choice&nbsp;
on Wizard’s part was simple:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:17.280
Sol Ring was considered by some to be a&nbsp;
must-include in the then-small Commander format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:22.140
since, on account of it being banned in&nbsp;
legacy, its low-double-digits price being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.180
pretty affordable for its powerful level. So,&nbsp;
the precon decks should also include Sol Ring.

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:34.560
I could say a lot about this logic—most&nbsp;
prominently that Sol Ring’s relative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:41.580
affordability is a product of it not being played&nbsp;
in large formats. Printing sets specifically for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:47.820
EDH was a great way to inflate the size of that&nbsp;
format, a process which, without a reprint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:53.940
would have caused a demand spike with a fixed&nbsp;
supply and increased the price, likely relegating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.940 --> 00:02:01.980
Sol Ring to higher power level EDH decks. It’s&nbsp;
a problem that solves itself, one could argue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:09.900
Also, the precon commander decks weren’t exactly&nbsp;
well-constructed, so the idea that they needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:16.500
to keep up with the decks of longtime&nbsp;
commander players is pretty laughable. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:20.820
something you might notice is that there’s a small&nbsp;
difference between me and Wizards of the Coast—I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:28.620
don’t make money off of how many units of the&nbsp;
commander precons decks get sold. And they do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.700
So, I guess I’m a little biased that way.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:42.420
But okay, I think it would have been fine, or even&nbsp;
advisable, to let Sol Ring’s price soar as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:49.440
format’s size grows—let it be a toy for the power&nbsp;
gamers, alongside Mana Crypt, Moxen, and the like.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:54.420
I understand why some people might&nbsp;
disagree, but that’s my opinion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.760
There are also people who have argued&nbsp;
that it should be banned, such as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:06.720
writer known as Chizarlicious two months after&nbsp;
the commander set’s release in 2011. And well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:11.820
unless somebody goes around burning every single&nbsp;
copy of Sol Ring on Earth released after 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:21.000
a ban would really be the only solution. But it’s&nbsp;
not going to happen. Commander’s ban list is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:29.160
hot take alert, kind of a stupid mess that very&nbsp;
clearly underscores the difficulty of moderating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:34.920
such a large format played in so many different&nbsp;
ways and with such a wide potential pool of cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:43.080
On top of this, Sol Ring has been printed&nbsp;
in over a dozen commander sets. Wizards have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:50.340
done their utmost to place the card within the&nbsp;
pantheon of EDH. So, Sol Ring is here to say.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:55.440
But what does that mean for the&nbsp;
game? What does the existence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:00.720
of wildly powerful cards at a cheap&nbsp;
price do to a format like commander?

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:10.860
One of the most compelling issues with these&nbsp;
cheap, powerful cards is that they warp the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:16.920
way that players look at other cards. Depending&nbsp;
on the goals of a deck and how it’s constructed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:24.900
it might be looking for varying quantities of mana&nbsp;
rocks, counter spells, draw spells, and so on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.540
You could build a framework for the deck without&nbsp;
looking at cards, for example going “okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:35.640
this deck wants eight ramp spells, ten&nbsp;
draw spells, and twelve removal spells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.940
I should look through cards to&nbsp;
find these quantities of cards.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:45.420
But c’mon, let’s be honest. This&nbsp;
is not how most people build decks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:51.240
People see their options for ramp, draw,&nbsp;
and removal, and just start adding cards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:57.000
The specific cards being considered then have&nbsp;
an impact on how many of that sort of card end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:03.780
up in the final deck. As a counter-scenario, one&nbsp;
can imagine a universe in which players’ removal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:09.360
options solely consist of Naturalize,&nbsp;
Murder, Lava Coil, and Banishing Light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:16.440
to be played in whatever quantities the player&nbsp;
wants. In this universe, a player is forced to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:21.000
spend more time thinking about questions like&nbsp;
“how much am I worried about enchantments and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:26.040
artifacts? How many creature removal spells do I&nbsp;
need to deal with opposing pressure and wincons?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:35.100
The question isn’t what specific removal a player&nbsp;
needs, but rather how much of these stock options.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:40.980
Back in the real world, things end up&nbsp;
working out a bit different than this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:48.180
This is especially pronounced if a player is using&nbsp;
a website like EDHRec, or some other metric that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:55.320
orders cards by popularity or quality. If you’re&nbsp;
looking for counterspells in scryfall ordered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:01.620
by EDHRec rank, as I have been spotted doing&nbsp;
from time to time, you first see Counterspell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:08.100
a card that’ll set you back a dollar.&nbsp;
Great, love it. Then you see a couple of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:12.960
other options that are similar with a downside,&nbsp;
as well as a couple that cost a lot of money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:19.500
By the time you get down to your standard&nbsp;
staples, your kitchen table kings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:27.120
your modern machinations of yore, it’s looking&nbsp;
a lot less tempting. You have your Mana Leak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:33.060
type cards that fall off in power fast in later&nbsp;
turns, you have your more specialized options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:39.120
and then you have your counterspells that&nbsp;
commit the cardinal sin of costing three mana.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:46.920
It’s easy to imagine a player, seeing the rapid&nbsp;
price increases and power decreases that happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:53.520
when you scroll downward from Counterspell,&nbsp;
going “well okay, I guess I’ll run Counterspell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:57.540
and a couple of these other ones, and then I’ll&nbsp;
have the good counterspells within my budget.”

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:04.500
Now, it should be mentioned that&nbsp;
Counterspell is an incredibly powerful card.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:10.140
Most other counter options that&nbsp;
players can run are worse than it, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:16.200
but I’d guess that most blue decks want&nbsp;
lowercase-c counterspells enough that they’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:22.200
benefit from running cards that are kind of a lot&nbsp;
worse than counterspell. A joke in my playgroup is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:28.140
to respond to the question of “oh what does that&nbsp;
card do?” with something like “oh yeah it’s like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:35.220
worse Sol Ring” or “oh yeah it’s basically a worse&nbsp;
version of Lightning Bolt.” The joke here is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:41.220
these statements tell you almost nothing about the&nbsp;
actual power of a card; it’s like somebody asking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:49.020
how old a person is you saying “oh yeah, they’re&nbsp;
younger than Henry Kissinger.” Helpful, thank you.

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:56.100
As a point of contrast, we can consider other&nbsp;
types of cards somebody might choose to run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:58.920
ones whose power is less obvious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:05.460
It’s pretty easy to go “oh, Wizard’s Retort?&nbsp;
That’s strictly worse than Counterspell.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:12.600
But what about if I want, say, a card that&nbsp;
generates value when I play creature spells?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:22.320
Alright… scryfall… let’s go green cards that have&nbsp;
“whenever”, “cast”, and “creature” in their text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:31.320
and let’s rank them based on EDHRec, and… there.&nbsp;
Well okay, so there’s Beast Whisperer, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:37.440
there’s The Great Henge, and there’s Silverback&nbsp;
Elder, and there’s Primeval Bounty, and there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:42.660
Glimpse of Nature, and there’s Wild Pair, and&nbsp;
there’s Masked Admirers, and there’s Vengevine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:49.680
So which ones here are the bad ones? Which ones&nbsp;
should I add, and where should I stop adding here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:56.340
These cards are pulled from all across the 57&nbsp;
ordered cards that pop up with my search inputs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:02.040
Some are more flexible or more&nbsp;
bursty or lower to the ground,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:08.460
but analyzing them is a much trickier value&nbsp;
proposition than looking at counterspells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:16.740
It’s possible I did the math on my deck ahead of&nbsp;
time and gone “okay, I need two or three of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:21.780
cards, preferably at least one in the 4-5 mana&nbsp;
range and at least one in the 6-7 mana range.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:28.740
But if I didn’t… gee, there sure are&nbsp;
a lot of good looking cards here…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:36.240
I could run worse Counterspell or worse&nbsp;
Sol Ring, or… I could run more of these.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:44.040
And counter spells aren’t the only thing to get&nbsp;
neglected. If I’m looking for creature removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:49.620
Swords to Plowshares and Path to Exile are&nbsp;
both under two bucks and absurdly powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.280 --> 00:09:54.360
with other white creature removal being&nbsp;
much worse or much more expensive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.620 --> 00:10:00.180
If I’m looking for artifact removal,&nbsp;
Red has Abrade and Vandalblast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:04.500
with other options once again&nbsp;
being much worse or more expensive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:11.040
If I’m looking for general purpose draw for&nbsp;
blue, there are a few low budget options—Mystic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.060 --> 00:10:17.160
Remora is fabulous, especially with its price&nbsp;
halved by a Dominaria Remastered reprint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:23.520
and Mulldrifter, Treasure Cruise, and Archmage&nbsp;
Emeritus all have decent spots in several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:30.240
different archetypes. But beyond that, things&nbsp;
get weaker, more specialized, or more expensive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:36.000
If somebody isn’t setting out a framework&nbsp;
for their deck, it’s easy to imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:41.400
under-doing it on the more generalist stuff&nbsp;
and overdoing it on the more synergistic stuff.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:47.400
And a deck running too much synergistic&nbsp;
stuff and not enough general stuff is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:52.260
at risk of getting itself into a bimodal&nbsp;
pickle—a state where it either falls on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:57.780
its face after getting a key piece removed,&nbsp;
or it goes on a mach 10 march toward victory.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:08.160
A basic issue with having cards like Sol Ring&nbsp;
widely available is that card quality in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:14.100
given deck gets a lot less consistent at lower&nbsp;
budgets. If you’re building a deck with a budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.100 --> 00:11:18.720
in the high hundreds or more, you might include&nbsp;
Counterspell alongside Mana Drain and a couple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:23.820
other pricy counters, along with whichever cheaper&nbsp;
ones best fit your deck and play situations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:30.540
If you needed some green ramp, you also might&nbsp;
include Nature’s Lore, along with Three Visits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:36.240
a clone from Portal Three Kingdoms, a set that&nbsp;
was almost exclusively sold in East Asian markets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:43.020
Until a series of reprints recently, the only&nbsp;
way to get this card was to pay upwards of $50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:50.340
for the P3K version. And honestly P3K could be&nbsp;
a video topic on its own, but I’m not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:56.640
dwell on it further today. All this to say,&nbsp;
shelling out money leads to deck consistency.

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:04.320
There are certainly powerful cards that are cheap&nbsp;
due to wide reprints or another reason, such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:10.140
Sol Ring, Counterspell, and Swords to Plowshares,&nbsp;
but there’s an even sneakier type of budget card:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:20.940
the ones that cost $0. A quick anecdote—I recently&nbsp;
built a Meria, Scholar of Antiquity deck. It’s an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:25.680
equipments deck—you throw a bunch of equipments&nbsp;
on Meria and then tap them to generate mana and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:32.460
value. I kind of built it as a meme but it’s a&nbsp;
genuinely delightful mid-power-level aggro deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:40.620
Anyway, after ordering the $40 worth of mostly&nbsp;
random bulk I needed for the list, I sat staring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:44.160
at the Sword of the Animist and the Sword of&nbsp;
Forge and Frontier I had in my collection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.600 --> 00:12:52.140
They cost no money to add and would be so good in&nbsp;
the deck. I put them in the deck for a few games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:57.660
but they had such a warping effect that&nbsp;
I decided to take them out. My deck’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.660 --> 00:13:02.640
mana and value engines were pretty solid for an&nbsp;
aggro deck, but decidedly reasonable in scope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.840 --> 00:13:08.340
While Explorer’s Scope and Rogue’s Gloves&nbsp;
both feel very appropriate within the list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:14.640
when I played Sword of Forge and Frontier&nbsp;
it felt like a totally different deck, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:19.080
know that the fact that that’s within the realm of&nbsp;
possibility naturally shifts the way my opponents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:24.900
will think about my deck. Ultimately, it wasn’t&nbsp;
meant to be a particularly high-powered deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.900 --> 00:13:30.900
nor could it hope of performing like a high-power&nbsp;
deck on the current budget. So I cut the Swords.

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:38.220
The point being made here is that you should&nbsp;
add cards to a deck under consideration of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:43.320
they’ll impact the games you play with the deck,&nbsp;
even if you own them and they’re free to add.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:50.220
My quasi-meme equipments deck has a handful of&nbsp;
specific weaknesses, and adding power cards to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:55.260
deck doesn’t help it manage those weaknesses with&nbsp;
any consistency, it just makes people wanna play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.260 --> 00:14:03.060
better decks against it. An even better example&nbsp;
is graveyard decks. Graveyard synergies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:10.440
wide-ranging, fun, and potentially quite powerful,&nbsp;
but they exist within a game with a lot of really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:17.280
heavy-duty graveyard hate. There are the options&nbsp;
like Tormod’s Crypt and Rest in Peace which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:24.180
straightforwardly demolish graveyard strategies,&nbsp;
and even cards like Nihil Spellbomb and Bojuka Bog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:30.360
which do so without even spending a card, with the&nbsp;
latter dodging counterspells on top of all that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.860 --> 00:14:37.260
All this to say, decks heavily based around&nbsp;
graveyards need to make a decision—accept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:43.860
the need to be resilient against this stuff,&nbsp;
usually by winning quickly or being loaded with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:49.620
counters for the hate, or play at a power&nbsp;
level where these cards are less common.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:57.480
This puts a lot of mid-power graveyard decks&nbsp;
in an awkward spot—adding stronger synergies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:03.120
will lead to crushing unprepared decks even more&nbsp;
than before, but will still end up with getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:08.400
wholesale dunked on by hate-cards unless the&nbsp;
efficiency of the deck is vastly improved to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:14.100
guarantee quicker or more abrupt wins. There’s&nbsp;
not necessarily a right or wrong answer here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:19.440
but my mantra is that if you don’t wanna play&nbsp;
higher power level, don’t play higher power level.

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:28.320
Another example of this is cheap infinite&nbsp;
combos—namely, Niv-Mizzet and Curiosity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:33.720
I’ve known people who went “well&nbsp;
this deck is running red and blue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:36.780
and I’ve got the cards sitting&nbsp;
right here, why not add them?”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:43.800
If you’re purely looking to increase the power&nbsp;
level of your deck, this is fine logic, but adding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:49.020
an infinite combo to your deck changes the whole&nbsp;
dynamic of the games once people know it’s there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:55.020
As a frequent enjoyer of aggro, I&nbsp;
often find myself asking the question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.860 --> 00:16:01.440
“how will each of my opponents be trying&nbsp;
to win the game?” If the answer goes from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:07.020
I dunno, drakes or something to an infinite&nbsp;
combo that requires very little setup,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:12.960
I will be hitting that player with my&nbsp;
creatures, and they will deserve it.

00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:18.660
As another anecdote about building decks&nbsp;
for the type of games you want to play,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:23.520
my brother recently got his first commander&nbsp;
deck, a decently-built Jodah deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:29.640
In a night with several EDH games, everybody&nbsp;
pulled out one of their more powerful lists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:35.160
every game he played the Jodah deck, resulting in&nbsp;
games where he was doing a lot but also eating a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.160 --> 00:16:39.240
lot of removal spells and needing to deal with&nbsp;
a bunch of powerful nonsense from other people.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:44.160
The games were close, but the only&nbsp;
game he actually won that night&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:48.000
was in a lower power level game&nbsp;
where he borrowed my Meria deck.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:53.340
At the end of the night, he proclaimed that&nbsp;
he needed to build a lower power level deck.

00:16:55.980 --> 00:17:01.140
To bring this scattered assortment of points back&nbsp;
down to Earth a little, let’s think about Sol Ring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:08.580
once again. A player running a budget deck might&nbsp;
run the ever-powerful Sol Ring alongside Hedron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.580 --> 00:17:15.060
Archive, lockets, or other slower mana ramp.&nbsp;
Now let’s consider this effect for draw—they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:18.840
running a single Consecrated Sphinx that&nbsp;
they happen to have in their collection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:23.760
alongside cards like Treasure Cruise,&nbsp;
Blue Sun’s Zenith, and Body of Knowledge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.320 --> 00:17:29.700
Add in a few other examples like this, and&nbsp;
it’s not hard to construct an outline for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:34.860
their deck where 10% of the time they chain very&nbsp;
powerful cards and explode the entire table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:41.460
but most of the rest of the time their deck&nbsp;
performs on a much lower caliber. Some might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:47.460
ask “well what’s the problem with this?”&nbsp;
And well, the answer to that is complicated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:52.920
because EDH is a complicated format with a&nbsp;
variety of different players and power levels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:59.940
and a format where table politics and the threat&nbsp;
perceptions of individual players play a key role.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:06.360
In this environment, individual power cards&nbsp;
might not necessarily pose a problem—y’know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:12.600
in my playgroups, Sol Ring is looked at&nbsp;
with a minor eye roll, a “nice draw bro.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.980 --> 00:18:19.260
If the Sol Ring player gets out of hand, you&nbsp;
hit them a bit and hopefully things calm down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.520 --> 00:18:23.520
Commander is theoretically&nbsp;
a self-balancing format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:28.320
but the more inconsistently powerful a deck&nbsp;
becomes, the trickier it becomes for other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:34.740
people to conceptualize the power level of that&nbsp;
deck. At a certain point, the self-balancing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:40.620
will simply be people hitting your deck, or&nbsp;
picking a more powerful deck to begin with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:45.540
because they don’t wanna contend with the&nbsp;
puzzle box that you’re presenting them with.

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:57.540
This video was a little meandering, so let’s try&nbsp;
and recap some of the key points here. Sol Ring is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.540 --> 00:19:03.780
an absurdly strong card, and I kinda hate putting&nbsp;
it in my lower power decks, but it’s not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.780 --> 00:19:09.060
get banned, nor would banning it particularly&nbsp;
constitute some kind of fundamental good for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.060 --> 00:19:16.320
the format in my opinion. Counterspell is also a&nbsp;
very strong card, and absolutely shouldn’t be held&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:23.340
as a power-level baseline for counter spells,&nbsp;
even if the card is called, well, Counterspell.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:31.500
If you’re looking for mana rocks, counterspells,&nbsp;
or really any other sort of supporting effect, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.500 --> 00:19:36.420
should ideally think about how many of these sorts&nbsp;
of effects your deck needs before going looking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:43.560
or at least be careful while looking at cards. A&nbsp;
deck built with the tools it needs at a slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:49.560
lower power level will perform well with much&nbsp;
more consistency than the deck that only picks the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:54.960
strongest 3-4 cards for categories of card where&nbsp;
the deck is really looking for more like 8 or 10.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.280 --> 00:20:01.440
An additional point I could flesh out more at a&nbsp;
later date is that sometimes the strongest option&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.440 --> 00:20:08.280
isn’t even the best for a deck. If I wanna drop&nbsp;
my 4 mana commander on curve as often as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:15.840
it might be correct to run some lower-power 1 and&nbsp;
2 drop mana ramp rather than Cultivate, Kodama’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:21.840
Reach, or Harrow. Similarly, some decks might&nbsp;
be much less worried about running 3 or 4 mana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:26.940
counter spells if they’re cheaper or better suited&nbsp;
to the deck than the options that cost less mana.

00:20:28.740 --> 00:20:33.840
Additionally, players should add cards to&nbsp;
their deck under consideration of how those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.840 --> 00:20:39.840
cards will fit into the sort of game they want&nbsp;
to play. I could add Torment of Hailfire to my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:43.860
turbo-battlecruiser Glissa deck, and&nbsp;
that would probably make it stronger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:49.380
but my opponents are going to view the deck wildly&nbsp;
differently if they know I have an I-win button.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.580 --> 00:20:55.020
Playing a truly absurd number of lands is the&nbsp;
goal of the deck and the fun part of the deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:59.040
and I don’t want people to feel like they’ll&nbsp;
get flattened instantly if they let me get to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:05.580
20 lands. Even if you own a card and it’s sitting&nbsp;
right there in front of you waiting to get added,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.580 --> 00:21:10.980
in a casual EDH group it might still be correct to&nbsp;
leave it out for the sake of keeping the deck at a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.980 --> 00:21:16.560
consistent power level. As mentioned earlier,&nbsp;
if you’re running a graveyard-centered deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:22.260
you could juice up the power level of your&nbsp;
graveyard synergies, but if you’re not prepared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.260 --> 00:21:28.440
deal with heavy-duty hate cards, you’re just going&nbsp;
to be running a mess of a deck that smashes fun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:32.460
casual decks but gets demolished at the&nbsp;
first sight of a Relic of Progenitus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:38.040
And, to bring things back to the earlier&nbsp;
points, if you’re running just the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:43.080
powerful of a certain effect like ramp,&nbsp;
removal, or draw, you’re going to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:47.880
handful of games where you get those and&nbsp;
pop off super hard, and other games where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:52.740
your deck is a clunky mass of high-cost synergy&nbsp;
cards that fails to accomplish much of anything.

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:59.040
But the bottom line is, if you&nbsp;
have a time machine and several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:01.680
million dollars for bribes, we&nbsp;
should go back in time together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:05.400
and prevent Wizards of the Coast from&nbsp;
printing Sol Ring in Commander 2011.

